From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 15:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm: dts: mediatek: keep the Echo Dot (5th gen)'s ADC out of
 shutdown

The TLV320ADC5140's reset line is a fixed regulator on the CPLD's ADC_RST
GPIO, which the device tree hands to the ADC as
reset-adc-regulator-supply. Only Amazon's ADC driver takes that supply;
mainline's tlv320adcx140 doesn't, so the regulator stayed disabled with
no users and the ADC in shutdown (read on a C2N6L4, 2026-11-26). With the
codec on its link (patch 0231) its component probe then failed with
-ENXIO and took the whole card down.

Keep the line high from boot. The regulator's 10 ms startup delay
covers the ADC's wake-up before anything talks to it.

---
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/mediatek/cannoli-cheesecake.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/mediatek/cannoli-cheesecake.dtsi
@@ -35,6 +35,14 @@
 		gpio = <&cpld AMZ_CPLD_GPIO_ADC_RST GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		startup-delay-us = <10000>;
 		enable-active-high;
+		/*
+		 * The ADC's reset line. Amazon's ADC driver takes this supply
+		 * (reset-adc-regulator-supply); mainline's tlv320adcx140 has no
+		 * such supply, so nothing enabled it and the ADC stayed in
+		 * shutdown, answering nothing on I2C (C2N6L4, 2026-11-26).
+		 */
+		regulator-always-on;
+		regulator-boot-on;
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 
